1
00:00:00,103 --> 00:00:05,224
Integrity under Pressure A supplier offered an employee a bribe to overlook

2
00:00:05,237 --> 00:00:10,224
a defective batch Accepting it would violate the company's rules and betray

3
00:00:10,237 --> 00:00:15,105
its clients Yet the employee feared that reporting the incident might endanger

4
00:00:15,118 --> 00:00:19,579
his promotion A colleague urged him to document what had happened and

5
00:00:19,592 --> 00:00:25,658
use the firm's reporting process The subsequent investigation found an irregularity in

6
00:00:25,671 --> 00:00:31,079
several transactions Managers had tried to whitewash the matter with an ambiguous

7
00:00:31,092 --> 00:00:36,526
statement but the evidence contradicted their denial The board decided to dismiss

8
00:00:36,539 --> 00:00:41,803
the responsible manager and commission an independent review It also promised to

9
00:00:41,816 --> 00:00:48,395
compensate affected customers A scandal can damage more than immediate earnings It

10
00:00:48,408 --> 00:00:54,224
can undermine the confidence on which long-term cooperation rests No organization can

11
00:00:54,237 --> 00:00:58,921
claim moral superiority merely because it has a code of conduct Its

12
00:00:58,934 --> 00:01:03,961
integrity becomes manifest when it is willing to acknowledge wrongdoing impose appropriate

13
00:01:03,974 --> 00:01:07,934
consequences and protect people who raise a legitimate concern
